window.AG_CHAPTERS["19"] = {"id":"19","title":"The Knee in the Wet Season","titleZh":"梅雨裡的膝","patient":{"name":"Ah-Shun the porter 挑夫阿順","sprite":"man"},"scene":{"img":"art/scene-treatment.jpg","key":"rain"},"pattern":"Damp-Heat Obstructing the Channels 濕熱痹阻","principle":"clear heat, drain damp, free the channels and stop the pain","tongue":"red body with a greasy yellow coating, thickest toward the root","pulseNote":{"en":"Rolling smoothly under the finger like beads on a tray, and hurrying.","zh":"往來流利如珠走盤，且數"},"needleAsk":{"en":"Needle <b>Y&#299;nl&#237;ngqu&#225;n SP9</b>. Tap its exact location.","zh":"行針 — 點出穴位精確位置"},"target":{"id":"SP9","zh":"陰陵泉","py":"Y&#299;nl&#237;ngqu&#225;n","x":193,"y":289,"img":"art/body-leg-medial.jpg","w":434,"h":1159,"view":{"x":36,"y":76,"w":232,"h":320},"decoys":[{"x":193,"y":372},{"x":145,"y":292},{"x":226,"y":286},{"x":193,"y":216},{"x":120,"y":400}],"note":"<b>陰陵泉 Y&#299;nl&#237;ngqu&#225;n SP9</b> — in the depression on the lower border of the medial condyle of the tibia, between the posterior border of the bone and the calf muscle. Sea point of the Spleen and the principal point for draining damp anywhere in the body; here it is also a local point for the knee, which is why it does two jobs at once in this case.<br>脛骨內側髁下緣凹陷中，脛骨後緣與腓腸肌之間。脾之合穴，通身祛濕之要穴；於本案又為膝之局部穴，故一穴而兼二用。"},"hints":{"ask":{"en":"Touch the knee before you ask anything about it. Your hand answers the first question.","zh":"未問之前，先以手按其膝。手先答第一問。"},"look":{"en":"Two things on this tongue, and they belong to two different pathogens. Name both.","zh":"此舌有二事，各屬一邪。當並名之。"},"pulse":{"en":"One quality tells you what has accumulated. The other tells you how hot it has become.","zh":"一象言其所積為何，一象言其熱之淺深。"},"dx":{"en":"Cold or hot first. Then which of the four kinds of bi. Then which of the two pathogens is winning.","zh":"先辨寒熱，次定四痹之屬，末審二邪孰勝。"},"rx":{"en":"One point drains damp and happens to sit on the knee. Take the coincidence.","zh":"有一穴，利濕而適在膝上。當取其兩得。"},"tech":{"en":"Hot, swollen, refusing the hand. There is nothing missing here.","zh":"紅腫灼熱而拒按。此案無所不足。"}},"reactions":[{"who":"master","en":"Master Shen lays the back of his hand on the knee and takes it away again at once.","zh":"參師父以手背按其膝，旋即抽手。"},{"who":"patient","en":"Ah-Shun has his trouser leg already rolled up. He has done this before.","zh":"阿順褲管早已捲起。他不是頭一回了。"},{"who":"boy","en":"The herb boy props the door open. The room has been closed all week and it smells of wet stone.","zh":"藥童把門撐開。屋子關了一旬，一股濕石頭的氣味。"},{"who":"master","en":"\"Fourteen days of rain and the third porter this week. Ask him what the weather does to it.\"","zh":"「雨下了十四天，這是這一旬第三個挑夫。問他天氣於其病何如。」"},{"who":"patient","en":"\"Worse when it is close and wet. A little better if I put river water on it.\"","zh":"「悶濕的時候更難受。澆點江水，倒鬆快些。」"},{"who":"master","en":"\"Better with cold. Write that down and remember which chapter it rules out.\"","zh":"「得涼則減。記下此語，並記其所排除者為第幾章。」"}],"remedy":{"ask":"pain_quality","look":"cold_heat","pulse":"cold_heat","dx":"cold_heat","rx":"channel_organ","tech":"excess_def"},"reward":{"cardEn":"Heat Inside the Damp","cardZh":"濕熱痹阻 · 清熱利濕通絡","cardTxt":"Bi means obstruction — wind, cold, damp or heat lodging in the channels and joints so that qi and blood cannot pass. Four kinds, and the differences are practical: wandering pain is wind (moving bi), severe fixed pain much better with warmth is cold (painful bi), heaviness and swelling that worsens in wet weather is damp (fixed bi), and a joint that is red, hot, swollen and better for cold is heat. Chapter two was the same category with the opposite thermal sign — cold-damp, warmed and moxaed. Here the damp has heat inside it, and everything that helped Old Wu would make this knee worse.","herbId":"yiyiren","herbEn":"Job's Tears Seed","herbZh":"薏苡仁","herbImg":"art/card-yiyiren.jpg","sprite":"art/sprite-renshen-jump.webp","herbTxt":"Sweet, bland and slightly cold, entering Spleen, Stomach and Lung. It percolates damp out through the urine, strengthens the Spleen so less damp is made, and specifically eases damp obstruction in the sinews — one of the few damp herbs that is gently cooling rather than warming, which is exactly what this pattern needs. Bland and slow, so it is used in quantity and over time. Traditionally used with caution in pregnancy."},"endings":{"three":["Ten days on, the knee is its own colour again and Ah-Shun is back on the dock. \"I keep the leg dry now,\" he says. \"And I stopped the wine.\"<br><b>Master Shen:</b> \"You had chapter two in your hand and you did not use it. Same category, opposite sign — and the moxa that saved the old boatman would have cooked this knee.\"","十日後，膝復其本色，阿順已回碼頭。「如今我腿上不教它濕，」他說，「酒也停了。」<br><b>參師父：</b>「第二章在你手中而你不用之。同類而反其寒熱——彼救老船家之艾灸，於此膝則熟之矣。」"],"two":["The swelling is down. It still aches when the rain comes.<br><b>Master Shen:</b> \"You took the heat out. Go back and ask which of the two was the larger half.\"","腫已消，逢雨猶痠。<br><b>參師父：</b>「熱已去矣。回頭問：二邪之中，孰居其多？」"],"one":["The knee is as it was, and he cannot carry a full load.<br><b>Master Shen:</b> \"You put your hand on it and it was hot. Everything followed from there and you went the other way.\"","膝如其舊，不能負全擔。<br><b>參師父：</b>「你手按其上，是熱的。一案自此而定，而你反其道行之。」"],"titles":["Ah-Shun Shoulders the Pole","Ah-Shun Carries Half a Load","Ah-Shun Waits for the Rain to Stop"]},"script":[{"w":"master","en":"You treated a cold, damp joint in your second week here. This is the same family and it will punish you for assuming.","cn":"你第二旬治過一個寒濕的關節。此與彼同類，然若以為同治，必受其咎。"},{"w":"patient","en":"I carry salt off the barges. Fourteen days of rain and my right knee has swelled up like a gourd.","cn":"我在船上扛鹽。雨下了十四天，右膝腫得像個葫蘆。"},{"w":"patient","en":"It is hot to touch. My wife says I am running a fever in one leg.","cn":"摸上去是燙的。內人說我一條腿在發燒。"},{"w":"patient","en":"And heavy. The whole leg feels like it has been filled with wet sand.","cn":"還沉。整條腿像灌了濕沙。"},{"w":"master","en":"Hot, swollen, heavy, in a wet season. Two pathogens, and you must decide which one you are looking at first.","cn":"熱、腫、重，而值濕令。此二邪也，當先定所視者為何。"}],"qa":[{"q":"May I put my hand on it? — What do you feel when I do?","cq":"容我按之。按之，你覺如何？","good":1,"a":"It burns under your hand. And do not press hard, please.","ca":"你手底下是燙的。還有——請別按重了。","clue":{"en":"Joint hot to the touch, refuses pressure","zh":"關節捫之灼手，拒按"},"note":"Two findings from one movement, and both are decisive. <b>Hot to the touch</b> settles the thermal question before a single question is asked — this is heat, whatever else is present. <b>Refusing pressure</b> settles excess versus deficiency. Learn to examine before you interview; the hand often answers faster than the patient. 一按而得二證，皆能決疑。<b>捫之灼手</b>，未問一語而寒熱已定：此有熱，餘者不論。<b>拒按</b>，則虛實亦定。當先診而後問；手之所答，每速於口。"},{"q":"Does warmth help, or cold?","cq":"得溫則舒？抑或得涼則舒？","good":1,"a":"Cold. River water on a cloth. Warmth makes it throb.","ca":"得涼。用布蘸江水敷著。一暖就跳著疼。","clue":{"en":"Relieved by cold, worse with warmth","zh":"得涼則減，得溫則甚"},"note":"The single most useful question in the whole of bi-syndrome, and the one that decides your technique. Cold bi is dramatically better with warmth — a heat pattern is worse with it. This one answer tells you moxa is contraindicated, which is the exact opposite of chapter two. 痹證諸問，此最有用，且足以定手法。寒痹得溫則大減，熱證則反甚。此一答即知本案禁灸——與第二章正相反。"},{"q":"Is the pain in one joint or does it move?","cq":"其痛在一節？抑或游走？","good":1,"a":"This knee. It started in the ankle two years ago but now it stays here.","ca":"就這個膝。兩年前起於足踝，如今只在此處。","clue":{"en":"Fixed to one joint","zh":"痛著一處不移"},"note":"Fixed points away from wind. Wandering pain that jumps from joint to joint is <i>moving bi</i>, and wind is what moves. Fixed, heavy, swollen pain is <i>fixed bi</i> — damp, which is heavy and settles. 固定者非風。痛走諸節而無定所者為行痹，風性善行故也。痛著一處而重而腫者為著痹——濕性重濁而下趨。"},{"q":"How does the weather change it?","cq":"天氣於其病何如？","good":1,"a":"Close, wet, still air is the worst. A dry wind and I can almost work.","ca":"悶、濕、無風之日最苦。若起乾風，倒還能做活。","clue":{"en":"Much worse in humid weather","zh":"陰濕天氣尤甚"},"note":"Damp confirmed from outside the patient. Bi patterns are among the few where the weather is a diagnostic instrument — humid worse means damp; cold worse means cold; and a patient who tracks the sky is giving you data, not superstition. 由外候而證濕。痹證為少數可以天時為診具者：陰濕而甚者濕，寒冷而甚者寒。病人能候天而知其病，此為據，非為誕。"},{"q":"Are you thirsty? And when you drink, do you want much?","cq":"渴否？飲則欲多否？","good":1,"a":"My mouth is dry all day but I do not want much. Two mouthfuls and I have had enough.","ca":"整日口乾，卻不想多喝。兩口就夠了。","clue":{"en":"Thirsty but no desire to drink much","zh":"口渴不欲多飲"},"note":"A small answer that carries a whole mechanism. Heat alone makes a patient want cold water in quantity. Here damp is already blocking the middle and holding fluid where it is not wanted, so the mouth is dry while the body has no room for more — <i>thirsty without desire to drink</i> is one of the classic markers of damp-heat, and it also tells you damp is a substantial part of the picture. 一小答而含一機。單熱者思冷飲而量多。今濕阻中焦，水停不化，故口雖乾而身無所容——「渴不欲飲」為濕熱之定證，且告你濕之分量不輕。"},{"q":"What colour is your urine, and how are your bowels?","cq":"小便何色？大便何如？","good":1,"a":"Dark yellow and not much of it. And the stool is sticky — it does not finish.","ca":"黃而短少。大便黏滯，總覺解不淨。","clue":{"en":"Scanty dark urine, sticky incomplete stool","zh":"小便短黃，大便黏滯不爽"},"note":"Both organs report the same pair. Dark scanty urine is heat; sticky stool that will not finish is damp — turbid, clinging, hard to shift. Damp-heat is usually easiest to confirm at the two lower gates, and it is where you will later judge whether treatment is working. 二便同報一對之邪。尿短黃者熱，便黏滯不爽者濕——濕性濁膩，故著而難去。濕熱之證，多以二便為最易驗處，日後亦於此驗其治之效否。"},{"q":"Have you had fever, or chills with it?","cq":"曾發熱否？兼惡寒否？","good":1,"a":"Warm in the afternoons, no shivering. And I sweat without it helping.","ca":"午後身熱，不寒戰。汗出而熱不解。","clue":{"en":"Afternoon fever without chills; sweating that does not resolve it","zh":"午後身熱不惡寒；汗出熱不解"},"note":"No chills means the pathogen is not on the surface — this is interior, not a fresh invasion. And sweating that fails to break the fever is characteristic of damp-heat: sweat clears heat, but damp is sticky and stays, so the heat rebuilds behind it. That single observation is often what names damp-heat rather than plain heat. 不惡寒者，邪不在表，其病在裡，非新感也。汗出而熱不解，濕熱之特徵：汗能泄熱，而濕黏滯不去，熱復生於其後。但憑此一觀，多足以別濕熱與單熱。"}],"look":[{"t":"Red body with a greasy yellow coating","c":"舌紅，苔黃膩","ok":1,"clue":{"en":"Red tongue, greasy yellow coat","zh":"舌紅苔黃膩"},"note":"Correct, and read it as two pathogens in one glance. <b>Yellow</b> is heat. <b>Greasy</b> — a coat that looks as though oil has been rubbed into it and cannot be scraped off — is damp or phlegm. Yellow and greasy together is damp-heat, and it is one of the fastest reads on the whole tongue. Which of the two is winning shows in the proportions: more yellow than greasy is heat leading; more greasy than yellow is damp leading. 是，一望而得二邪。<b>黃</b>主熱。<b>膩</b>者，苔如油膩之所塗，刮之不去，主濕主痰。黃而膩者濕熱，舌診中最捷之一讀。二邪孰勝，則觀其分：黃勝於膩者熱重，膩勝於黃者濕重。"},{"t":"Pale and swollen with a white slippery coat","c":"舌淡胖，苔白滑","ok":0,"note":"Cold damp — chapter two, and chapter fifteen. Damp is right and the temperature is exactly wrong; white and slippery is damp without heat, and treating this knee as if it were that one means moxa on a joint that is already burning. 寒濕之舌，第二章、第十五章是也。濕是而寒熱大謬：白滑者濕而無熱。以彼法治此膝，則灸其已灼者。"},{"t":"Red with no coating","c":"舌紅無苔","ok":0,"note":"Empty heat from yin deficiency. There is nothing empty here — thick greasy coat, swollen hot joint, forceful rolling pulse. A missing coat means missing substance; his has too much of something, not too little. 陰虛虛熱之舌。本案無虛：苔厚膩、關節腫熱、脈滑有力。無苔者形質不足；本案有餘，非不足。"},{"t":"Dark purple with stasis spots","c":"舌紫暗有瘀點","ok":0,"note":"Blood stasis — yesterday&#39;s chapter. Long-standing bi does eventually involve stasis, and in a chronic case you would expect to see it. Two years in, his tongue has not gone there yet. 血瘀之舌，昨日之章。痹久誠可入血成瘀，久病當察之。本案二年，其舌未至於此。"}],"pulse":[{"t":"Slippery and rapid 滑數","ok":1,"clue":{"en":"Slippery rapid pulse","zh":"脈滑數"},"note":"Correct, and again two pathogens in two syllables. <b>Slippery</b> — rolling smoothly under the finger like beads on a tray — means something substantial has accumulated: damp, phlegm or food. <b>Rapid</b> means heat. Slippery-and-rapid is the standard pulse of damp-heat, and it matches the tongue exactly, which is what confirmation looks like. 是，又二字而及二邪。<b>滑</b>者往來流利如珠走盤，主有形之積：濕、痰、食。<b>數</b>者主熱。滑數者，濕熱之常脈，與舌相符——所謂脈證相合者，此也。"},{"t":"Choppy 澀","ok":0,"note":"The opposite quality, and yesterday&#39;s pulse. Choppy catches and hesitates; slippery rolls. If you cannot yet feel the difference, that is the single most useful thing to practise on every wrist you touch. 其象適反，昨日之脈也。澀者艱滯，滑者流利。若未能辨之，則凡按一腕，皆當以此為習——診法之中，此最有用。"},{"t":"Deep, slow and weak 沉遲無力","ok":0,"note":"Yang deficiency. Slow says cold and weak says empty; this knee is hot and this pulse has force. 陽虛之脈。遲者寒，無力者虛。本膝灼熱，本脈有力。"},{"t":"Floating and tight 浮緊","ok":0,"note":"Wind-cold on the surface. He has no chills, no aversion to wind, and this has run two years. 表有風寒。本案不惡寒、不惡風，且病已二年。"}],"dx":[{"ask":"Step 1 — cold or hot? Decide before anything else.<br><small class=\"zh\" style=\"color:var(--ink-soft)\">第一步：寒乎？熱乎？此當先決。</small>","opts":[{"t":"Hot — the joint is red, hot, swollen, and better for cold","c":"熱 — 關節紅腫灼熱，得涼則減","ok":1,"note":"Correct, and four independent findings agree: the joint burns under your hand, it is visibly red, cold relieves it and warmth makes it throb. Any one might be argued with; all four together end the question. This decision comes first because it decides the technique — get it wrong and you will moxa a joint that needs cooling, which is not a small error but the whole treatment backwards. 是，四證獨立而同歸：捫之灼手、望之色赤、得涼而減、得溫而甚。有其一猶可議，四者俱見則無可疑。此當先決者，以其決手法也：誤之則灸其當清者，非小失，乃通治盡反。"},{"t":"Cold — it is a joint complaint in the wet season","c":"寒 — 濕令而病關節","ok":0,"note":"Season is context, not diagnosis. Damp weather produces cold-damp bi and damp-heat bi in equal measure, and the difference is in the patient, not the sky. You touched the knee and it burned. 時令為境，非診斷也。濕令之中，寒濕與濕熱並生，其別在人不在天。你手按其膝，是灼的。"},{"t":"Neither — it is a deficiency of the sinews","c":"非寒非熱 — 筋失所養","ok":0,"note":"Deficient sinews are weak, thin and slack, and the pain welcomes a hand. This joint is swollen, hot and refuses to be touched. 筋失養者，痿弱瘦削而喜按。本節腫熱而拒按。"},{"t":"Both, equally","c":"寒熱錯雜，二者並重","ok":0,"note":"Mixed cold and heat happens — chronic bi with cold in one joint and heat in another, or heat above with cold below. It requires evidence of both, and there is no cold sign anywhere in this case. 寒熱錯雜誠有之：久痹或一節寒而一節熱，或上熱而下寒。然必二者俱有其徵，本案無一寒之候。"}]},{"ask":"Step 2 — bi has four kinds. Which is this?<br><small class=\"zh\" style=\"color:var(--ink-soft)\">第二步：痹有四證。此屬何者？</small>","opts":[{"t":"Heat bi, with damp — fixed, swollen, hot, worse in humidity","c":"熱痹兼濕 — 著而不移，腫、熱、遇濕則甚","ok":1,"note":"Correct. Run the four: <b>moving bi</b> is wind — pain that jumps from joint to joint, and his does not. <b>Painful bi</b> is cold — severe fixed pain dramatically relieved by warmth, and warmth makes his worse. <b>Fixed bi</b> is damp — heavy, swollen, worse in wet weather, and that half fits him exactly. <b>Heat bi</b> is red, hot, swollen and better for cold, and that half fits him too. So: damp with heat in it. Naming both is not indecision; it is the diagnosis. 是。試以四者次之：<b>行痹</b>屬風，痛走諸節，本案不然。<b>痛痹</b>屬寒，痛劇而得溫大減，本案得溫反甚。<b>著痹</b>屬濕，重、腫、遇陰濕則甚，此半正合。<b>熱痹</b>紅腫灼熱而喜涼，此半亦合。故為濕中有熱。並舉其二，非兩可也，乃其診也。"},{"t":"Painful bi — cold predominating","c":"痛痹 — 寒邪偏勝","ok":0,"note":"Cold bi is the one that answers to warmth, and answers to it unmistakably — a patient with cold bi will tell you a hot cloth is the only thing that helps. His asks for river water. 痛痹者得溫則減，且其效甚著——寒痹之人必言熱敷為惟一可恃者。本案所求者江水也。"},{"t":"Moving bi — wind predominating","c":"行痹 — 風邪偏勝","ok":0,"note":"Wind moves, and so does the pain it causes: shoulder one week, wrist the next. His has been in one knee for two years. 風性善行，其痛亦然：今在肩，旬日在腕。本案二年止在一膝。"},{"t":"Bony bi from long-standing deficiency","c":"骨痹 — 久虛所致","ok":0,"note":"Chronic bi does deplete the Liver and Kidney over years, and in an older patient with deformed, aching, weak joints you would treat that. His is an acute hot flare in a working man of thirty-one. Treat what is in front of you first. 痹久誠傷肝腎，年老而關節變形、痠軟無力者，當治其虛。本案為三十一歲力作之人，其病為急性熱發。當先治目前所見。"}]},{"ask":"Step 3 — damp and heat are both here. Which one is the larger half, and how can you tell?<br><small class=\"zh\" style=\"color:var(--ink-soft)\">第三步：濕熱俱在。孰居其多？何以知之？</small>","opts":[{"t":"Damp leads — the coat is more greasy than yellow, the stool is sticky, the leg is heavy, and he is thirsty without wanting to drink","c":"濕重於熱 — 苔膩多於黃，便黏，肢重，渴不欲飲","ok":1,"note":"Correct, and this is the judgement that shapes the prescription. The markers of <b>damp leading</b>: a coat more greasy than yellow, heaviness, swelling, sticky unfinished stool, thirst without desire to drink, fever that does not break with sweating. The markers of <b>heat leading</b>: a coat more yellow than greasy, high fever, real thirst for cold drinks in quantity, dark scanty urine, a bounding rapid pulse, marked irritability. He has almost the whole first list. So: drain damp as the main line and clear heat alongside it — a formula built the other way round would be cold and cloying, and cold cloying medicine makes damp worse. 是。此判所以定其方也。<b>濕重</b>之候：苔膩多於黃、身重、腫、便黏不爽、渴不欲飲、汗出熱不解。<b>熱重</b>之候：苔黃多於膩、壯熱、真渴而喜冷飲且量多、尿短赤、脈洪數、煩躁甚。本案幾具前列之全。故當以利濕為主，清熱佐之。若反其序，則方偏寒膩，而寒膩之品適助其濕。"},{"t":"Heat leads — the joint is hot and the tongue is red","c":"熱重於濕 — 節熱而舌紅","ok":0,"note":"Heat is certainly present, but present is not predominant. Weigh it: no high fever, no real thirst, a coat more greasy than yellow, and stool that is sticky rather than dry and bound. Treat this as heat-led and you will reach for heavy cold bitter herbs, which are cloying, which is precisely what damp does not need. 熱誠有之，然「有」非「勝」。權之：無壯熱、無真渴、苔膩多於黃、便黏而不燥結。若以熱重治之，必投重寒苦之品；寒苦滋膩，正濕之所忌。"},{"t":"They are exactly equal","c":"二者適均","ok":0,"note":"Sometimes true, and here it is a way of not deciding. The evidence is not balanced: count the damp signs and the heat signs and the first list is longer. Making the call is what turns a pattern name into a prescription. 有時誠然，而於此則所以避決耳。其據不均：數濕之證與熱之證，前者為多。能決之，乃可由證而及方。"},{"t":"The question does not change the treatment","c":"孰勝於治無涉","ok":0,"note":"It changes it entirely. Damp-led calls for aromatic, bland, percolating, drying herbs with a little cooling; heat-led calls for cold bitter herbs with a little damp-draining. The same two words in the pattern name, two different prescriptions. 其涉甚大。濕重者，宜芳化、淡滲、苦燥，而少佐以清；熱重者，宜苦寒，而少佐以利濕。證名二字雖同，其方迥異。"}]}],"rx":[{"id":"SP9","t":"Y&#299;nl&#237;ngqu&#225;n SP9 陰陵泉","ok":1,"note":"The Sea point of the Spleen and the principal point for draining damp anywhere in the body — and it happens to sit on the medial side of this knee, so it is a local point and the pattern point in one needle. When a distal indication and a local one coincide, take the coincidence; that is what makes a point choice elegant rather than merely correct. 脾之合穴，通身利濕之要穴——而其位適在本膝之內側，一針而兼局部與辨證二用。遠道之主治與局部之所需相值，則取其兩得：此穴之選所以為工，不徒為是而已。"},{"id":"ST36","t":"Z&#250;s&#257;nl&#464; ST36 足三里","ok":1,"note":"Here it is not tonifying so much as regulating: the Spleen makes damp when it cannot transform, and the Stomach&#39;s Sea point strengthens that transforming function so less damp is produced. It also sits near the knee. In an acute hot flare use it moderately — the aim is to stop damp being made, not to add fuel. 於此非徒補也，兼調之：脾不運則生濕，取胃合穴以助其運，則濕不復生。且其位近膝。急性熱發之際，用之宜緩——所求者絕濕之源，非增其資也。"},{"id":"GB34","t":"Y&#225;ngl&#237;ngqu&#225;n GB34 陽陵泉","ok":1,"note":"The Influential point of the sinews and the Sea point of the Gallbladder, sitting on the lateral side of the same knee — so it pairs with SP9 across the joint, one needle either side. For any disorder of the sinews and for knee disorders in particular this is the standard partner. 筋會，膽之合穴，位在本膝之外側——與陰陵泉隔膝相對，內外各一針。凡筋之為病，尤其膝疾，此為常配。"},{"id":"KI7","t":"F&#249;li&#363; KI7 復溜","ok":0,"note":"Warms Kidney yang and moves water. It does move water — but it warms while doing it, and warmth is the one thing this joint has told you it cannot have. 溫腎陽而行水。行水誠是，然其行也以溫；而此膝所明言不受者，正溫也。"},{"id":"PC4","t":"X&#236;m&#233;n PC4 郄門","ok":0,"note":"Yesterday&#39;s point — the Pericardium&#39;s cleft point, for sudden severe chest pain and palpitations. Right rule, wrong region entirely. 昨日之穴：心包郄穴，主暴發心胸痛與心悸。其例不謬，而部位全非。"},{"id":"LU7","t":"Li&#232;qu&#275; LU7 列缺","ok":0,"note":"A distal point for the head and neck, and a confluent point of the Ren mai. Nothing in this case is above the waist. 治頭項之遠端穴，八脈交會通任脈。本案無一證在腰以上。"}],"tech":[{"t":"Reducing method, and no moxa","c":"瀉法，禁灸","ok":1,"note":"<b>Correct, and the no-moxa half is the one to hold on to.</b> Reduce, because everything here is full: a swollen joint that refuses the hand, a forceful slippery pulse, a thick coat. And refuse the moxa, because there is heat — the patient told you himself that warmth makes it throb. This is the exact mirror of chapter two, where the same category of illness was cold and moxa was the treatment. Same family, opposite sign, opposite technique. 是，而所當謹守者在「禁灸」。瀉者，通案皆實：關節腫而拒按、脈滑有力、苔厚。禁灸者，有熱也——病人自言得溫則跳痛。此與第二章適成鏡照：彼同類而屬寒，灸為其治。同家而反其寒熱，則其法亦反。"},{"t":"Reducing method, with moxa to move the damp","c":"瀉法，加灸以化其濕","ok":0,"note":"The instinct is understandable — moxa does transform damp, and it was right in chapter two. But damp with heat inside it is a different animal: warm the damp and you concentrate the heat inside it, and the joint swells further. When damp and heat travel together, the heat sets the rule for warmth. 其意可解——灸誠能化濕，第二章正用之。然濕中有熱者，其類不同：溫其濕則助其中之熱，而腫益甚。凡濕熱並見，溫法之可否，決於熱而不決於濕。"},{"t":"Reinforcing method — he is a labourer and worn out","c":"補法 — 其人力作而疲","ok":0,"note":"Tired is not deficient. Read the three: the joint refuses pressure, the pulse has force, the coat is thick. Every one of them says excess, and supplementing an excess pattern feeds the pathogen. 倦非即虛。察其三：拒按、脈有力、苔厚——皆言其實。實而補之，是養其邪。"},{"t":"Even method, to be gentle with an inflamed joint","c":"平補平瀉，以其節腫而從緩","ok":0,"note":"Gentleness is right at the joint itself — needle the hot swollen knee shallowly and do not manipulate hard. But gentleness of <i>handling</i> is not the same as even <i>technique</i>: the pattern is full, so the method is reducing, delivered with a light hand. 於患處誠當從緩：腫熱之膝，宜淺刺而勿重手法。然「手輕」非「法平」：其證為實，故法為瀉，特行之以輕手耳。"}]};
